 "text": "Hello, how are you holding up? It depends how you count. Both languages share common ancestors, so some words look the same. But the grammar is very different. In English, words follow their rules, but in Dutch and Norwegian, verbs follow the words they govern. That can take getting used to. It is, once you realise you don't need verbs at the beginning of sentences. Dutch and Norwegian sound pretty similar to English ears, because they use the same vowels. The big difference is often the consonants. It depends what you like. Dutch is shorter, because it' older. Norwegian is longer, because it' still growing. If you like the sound of it, go for it. Both languages are very friendly to learners, because so many English speakers live there.",
